  "read": {
    "title": "Two striking arts, two scoring systems",
    "text": [
      "Karate grew up in Okinawa, where local fighting methods mixed with Chinese influences, and reached mainland Japan in the early twentieth century through teachers such as Gichin Funakoshi, founder of Shotokan. Its training rests on three pillars: kihon, the basics drilled over and over; kata, prearranged solo sequences that store technique and principle; and kumite, sparring.",
      "World Karate Federation kumite rewards control and clean contact. A punch scores yuko, 1 point; a kick to the body scores waza-ari, 2; a kick to the head, or a score on a thrown or fallen opponent, earns ippon, 3. If points are level, senshu gives the edge to whoever scored first unopposed.",
      "Taekwondo, a Korean art whose name means the way of foot and fist, is best known for fast, high kicks. Students practice poomsae, the forms, and kyorugi, sparring. In World Taekwondo competition, electronic sensors help register hits: a punch to the trunk protector scores 1 point, a kick to it 2, and a kick to the head 3, with more for spinning kicks. Each gam-jeom penalty hands the opponent a point."
    ],
